---
/**
 * About (spec 6.1): two paragraphs — what this is, and why store reviews are a
 * better evidence base than forum posts for this particular question.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { getProducts } from '../lib/data';

const products = getProducts();
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="What Friction is, and why app store reviews are a better evidence base than forum posts for finding recurring product problems."
>
  <div class="shell prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      Friction is a free, public index of recurring complaints about widely used mobile apps. It
      reads publicly visible App Store and Google Play reviews for {products.length} apps, groups
      the ones describing the same underlying problem, scores each problem from evidence, and
      publishes the working. It is built for product designers and PMs who want evidence of what is
      actually broken in a product, and for founders deciding whether a problem is worth solving.
      There is no signup, no payment and nothing to buy.
    </p>

    <p>
      Store reviews are a better evidence base than forum posts for this question, and the reason is
      metadata. A forum thread tells you someone was annoyed. A store review tells you the star
      rating, the app version, the country and the date. That means this project can answer
      questions a forum-based tool structurally cannot: whether a complaint started after a specific
      release, whether it affects one platform or both, and whether it is getting worse or fading.
      Those are the questions that decide whether a problem is worth anyone's time. The cost of that
      metadata is that individual reviews have no permanent public link, which the{' '}
      <a href={url('/methodology')}>methodology</a> is honest about.
    </p>

    <p class="sig">
      Built by <a href="https://github.com/Jayharwani" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jay Harwani</a>.
      The <a href="https://github.com/Jayharwani/friction" rel="noopener noreferrer">source and
      every version of the data</a> are public.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose {
    padding-block: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .prose > * {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  p {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  .sig {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
